1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:01,500
Here's MatchPulse Live.

2
00:00:01,740 --> 00:00:06,080
This demo runs on Azure Container Apps
and each match event is synthetic.

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:09,400
We're following Northbridge City
against Harbor United,

4
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:11,400
a seeded and fictional matchup.

5
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:14,520
Accepted events become
evidence-linked match insights.

6
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,280
Nothing here comes from live
external data.

7
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:20,900
Every match moment links
to accepted event evidence.

8
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:24,060
That clear source supports
the insight you see,

9
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:27,620
and keeps the story grounded in what
the system accepted too.

10
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:32,280
Northbridge City gets on the board
from a synthetic event the system accepts.

11
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:37,620
That accepted moment supports the insight,
and its evidence stays attached.

12
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:39,280
Same event twice?

13
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:42,480
The score stays put,
and the duplicate changes nothing.

14
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:46,360
The replay doesn't add another
goal or change the accepted result.

15
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:50,560
An out-of-order event is rejected
cleanly, without corrupting match state.

16
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:52,980
Match state stays consistent too.

17
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:57,780
Fan view makes the verified moment
immediate and easy to follow.

18
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:02,200
It stays clear at a glance, still
grounded in the accepted match evidence.

19
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,500
Analyst view lays out the calculation,
inputs and evidence identifiers,

20
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:11,980
so you can trace how the insight was
formed back to accepted event evidence.

21
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:16,260
Broadcaster view condenses that
same insight for a live overlay,

22
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:19,620
presenting the same match facts
in less space for a live audience.

23
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:22,960
In English, Spanish or French,
the wording changes,

24
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:26,540
but the same match evidence
stays in every version too.

25
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:30,380
Northbridge City beats
Harbor United, two goals to one.

26
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:33,340
The recap is grounded
in accepted match events,

27
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:37,880
linking this result to the events the
system accepted rather than outside data.

28
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:40,240
For this deployment,

29
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:43,080
every result comes
from deterministic fallback.

30
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:47,460
Foundry is disabled in the hosted
runtime, so model calls are zero.

31
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:52,300
Hosted on Azure, this demo uses
synthetic data, not a live feed.

32
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:54,090
This is a fictional match.
